                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the fracture`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Day one of the merged Lyon hub. On-time dispatch has fallen from 96% to <strong>81%</strong> in 72 hours; the two legacy teams are hoarding dock equipment and missing cross-team handoffs. A pulse shows perceived fairness at <strong>38</strong> among legacy Vélocité staff versus <strong>61</strong> among Septentrion — the south already believes the deal is rigged. <strong>18%</strong> of staff intend to leave within six months, the most mobile first. Separate the <em>operational symptom</em> (dispatch collapse) from its <em>root cause</em> (a justice-and-cooperation breakdown between two distrustful groups), and recognise that your own early signals — a northern director, the northern IT system, the first two promotions north — are part of the problem. Name the single behaviour you must model first.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Design the communication & fairness plan`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Build the first 6 weeks of integration leadership. <strong>(a) Communication:</strong> in a merger, silence is not neutral — it is read as something to hide, and the rumour mill fills the vacuum. <strong>(b) Fairness signals:</strong> you have 10 high-visibility moves to allocate. Fairness is read through <em>procedure</em> (how decisions are made) and <em>interaction</em> (the respect and consistency you show) as much as through outcomes. A balanced leadership team, a jointly designed roster, and visible consistency between what you ask and what you do carry more weight than equal training alone — and every euro of headline perks for leadership is an anti-signal. Tie each move to its intended effect on fairness and cooperation.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Execute the redundancy & model behaviour under fire`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The hard decision lands: <strong>70 of 600 roles</strong> must go. People will accept a hard outcome from a <em>fair process</em> and reject a soft outcome from an unfair one — a generous severance decided behind closed doors spends the procedural fairness that keeps the surviving 530 cooperating. Decide what you personally model: deliver the news directly or delegate it; share the pain or protect your perks; apply the criteria evenly across both legacies or quietly protect ‘your own’. Mid-round, a rumour spreads that the cuts fall on the south, a peak-shift walkout is threatened, and a star dispatcher resigns. Hold cooperation and operations together in real time <strong>without breaking the fairness you built</strong>.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover cooperation & set the new norm`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present to group leadership and the employee-voice representative. Report the four KPIs — <strong>cooperation, fairness, change adoption, turnover risk</strong> — against the day-one baseline, and justify your trade-offs honestly (“we slowed the redundancy by two weeks to run a fair voice process, accepting a delay in synergy capture”). Then state how the combined hub will <em>sustain</em> cooperation: bonuses and tighter supervision buy compliance, not cooperation — only embedded fairness mechanisms and sustained exemplary behaviour make two workforces one team. Make the case for why the Lyon hub will hit 95% dispatch through peak.`,

                    r1_diag_label:          `What is actually breaking dispatch at the Lyon hub?`,
                    r1_signal_label:        `Which fairness signal is already misfiring the most?`,
                    r1_model_label:         `The single behaviour you must model first (%) — how visibly you commit to it`,
                    r1_model_readout:       `Visible commitment to model even-handedness first:`,
                    r1_model_hint:          `In a merger there is no neutral behaviour under scrutiny — every move is read as a signal of who the deal favours.`,
                    diag_justice:           `A justice-and-cooperation breakdown — two distrustful legacies won't cooperate, and my own early signals are part of the cause`,
                    diag_hands:             `An operations capacity problem — the merged hub is short of hands; add supervisors and overtime to push dispatch back up`,
                    diag_it:                `A systems problem — the new IT rollout is slowing everyone; freeze process changes until the software settles`,
                    diag_north:             `A performance problem — the Vélocité teams are slower; staff the best shifts with Septentrion people who hit the numbers`,
                    signal_early:           `My own early decisions — northern director, northern IT, first two promotions north — read as a rigged deal`,
                    signal_pay:             `Pay — the two legacies are on different pay scales`,
                    signal_rumor:           `The rumour mill — gossip is the only real problem`,
                    signal_none:            `None yet — fairness perceptions are fine, this is purely operational`,

