                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_justice:       `<strong>That is the right diagnosis.</strong> The dispatch drop is the symptom; the disease is two distrustful legacies refusing to cooperate — and your own early signals are part of why. You cannot supervise your way out of a justice problem. Now name the behaviour you will model first, because in a merger there is no neutral.`,
                    coach_r1_hands:         `<strong>You threw supervisors and overtime at the dispatch problem.</strong> But these teams aren't failing because they lack hands — they're failing because they won't cooperate. Vélocité staff rate fairness at 38 and are hoarding the docks. What broke between these two groups, and what are <em>you</em> signalling?`,
                    coach_r1_it:            `<strong>Freezing the IT rollout treats a justice problem as a software problem.</strong> The new system being Septentrion's is one of the signals that hardened the south's distrust — but freezing it won't make 360 Vélocité staff trust the deal. The handoffs are failing because cooperation broke, not because of a login screen.`,
                    coach_r1_north:         `<strong>You staffed the best shifts with Septentrion people because their day-one numbers looked better.</strong> The southern workforce just confirmed its worst fear, and cooperation at the seams got worse. In the first 90 days, perceived even-handedness across legacies matters more than rewarding a number you can't yet trust. What signal did that send to 360 Vélocité employees?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the justice-and-cooperation root cause AND saw that your own early decisions — the northern director, the northern IT, the promotions north — are the misfiring signal. That is the lens that lets everything you do next read as fair rather than rigged.`,
                    coach_r1_signalnone:    `<strong>‘Fairness perceptions are fine’ is exactly what the south is watching you fail to notice.</strong> A 23-point gap between the legacies on day one is not noise — it is the fracture that is breaking your handoffs. Treat it as operational only and it will quietly empty your most mobile talent out the door.`,
                    coach_r1_lowmodel:      `<strong>Low visible commitment to modelling even-handedness is itself a signal.</strong> In a merger every move is read; if you won't visibly commit to fairness first, the workforce reads hesitation as favouritism. There is no neutral behaviour under this much scrutiny.`,
                    coach_r2_honest:        `<strong>Honest communication is a fairness signal in itself.</strong> Naming the ‘merger of equals’ reality and running mixed-team listening sessions tells both legacies the deal is being made in the open. An honest, incomplete message beats a polished silence every time.`,
                    coach_r2_memo:          `<strong>A one-way memo from HQ reassures no one who is watching for fairness.</strong> It is communication without voice — the south hears a northern HQ talking at them, not listening. Mixed-team listening and transparent criteria do far more than a glossy all-hands.`,
                    coach_r2_silence:       `<strong>You chose silence until the plan is final.</strong> In the vacuum the rumour mill has already decided the cuts fall on the south, fairness has dropped further, and your best dispatchers are interviewing elsewhere. Silence is not neutral in a merger — it is read as something to hide.`,
                    coach_r2_perks:         `<strong>You spent your fairness signals on headline perks and an office refit for leadership.</strong> Every driver can see that gap. You are asking for new rosters and a 12% cut while visibly protecting leadership status — that is the loudest anti-signal in the building. What are you modelling?`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A procedurally weighted allocation, honestly communicated, is the credible play.</strong> Balanced leadership, a jointly designed roster, and visible consistency between your ask and your action tell both legacies the process is fair — and procedural fairness is what holds cooperation, not perks.`,
                    coach_r3_fair:          `<strong>A fair process for a painful outcome is exactly right.</strong> Transparent criteria, employee voice, and real support mean the surviving 530 see a process they can trust even as 70 roles go. People accept a hard outcome from a fair process and reject a soft one from an unfair process.`,
                    coach_r3_closed:        `<strong>You designed a generous severance — good. But you selected the 70 behind closed doors, no criteria, no voice.</strong> You bought distributive fairness and spent procedural fairness. Which one actually keeps the other 530 cooperating? The package soothes the leavers; the process is what the survivors are watching.`,
                    coach_r3_fast:          `<strong>You executed all 70 cuts in week 2 to bank the synergy early.</strong> You hit the number — and cratered fairness and cooperation, and dispatch is still stuck because the survivors have checked out. Speed on the outcome destroyed the process that keeps the operation running. Was two weeks of saved synergy worth the cooperation you spent?`,
                    coach_r3_delegate:      `<strong>You delegated the bad news, kept your perks, and shielded ‘your own’.</strong> Every employee can see the gap between what you ask and what you do. In a merger your behaviour is the loudest message in the building — why would anyone sacrifice when you visibly won't?`,
                    coach_r3_protect:       `<strong>You quietly protected the legacy you trust more.</strong> The southern workforce's worst fear is now confirmed in the one decision that mattered most. Even-handedness across legacies in the first 90 days is worth more than any number you think you're protecting — and you just spent it.`,
                    coach_r3_address:       `<strong>That is exemplary crisis leadership.</strong> Showing the audited criteria to the floor in person, hearing the walkout's concern, and personally retaining the star dispatcher with a fair conversation protected both the people and the dispatch — in real time, without breaking the fairness you built.`,
                    coach_r3_suppress:      `<strong>A firm denial memo and a contract-breach warning is procedural force, not procedural fairness.</strong> Threatening people who feel wronged confirms the rumour rather than answering it. Show them the criteria; don't warn them into silence.`,
                    coach_r3_inject_ignore: `<strong>You pushed the shift through and ignored the rumour and the resignation.</strong> The south now believes the cuts fell on them, the walkout sentiment hardened, and the dispatcher you couldn't afford to lose is gone. A rumour ignored in a merger is a rumour confirmed.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>An honest scorecard against the baseline is the durable case.</strong> Reporting all four KPIs and owning the trade-offs you made is what earns the credibility to lead the combined hub forward — and it is the only version group leadership and the employee rep can both believe.`,
                    coach_r4_dispatch:      `<strong>Leading with dispatch alone tells leadership the people problem is solved when it isn't.</strong> The number recovered because cooperation did — push the people story to HR and you lose the very thing holding the dispatch up. The four KPIs are one story.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Claiming ‘fully integrated’ while a fairness gap remains is spin, and the employee rep is in the room.</strong> Two workforces don't become one because you said so — overselling the recovery is how you lose the trust you just started to build.`,
                    coach_r4_norms:         `<strong>Embedded fairness mechanisms and sustained exemplary behaviour are what make two workforces one team.</strong> Bonuses and surveillance buy compliance for a quarter; norms and modelled leadership outlast the crisis. That is the operating model that holds 95% dispatch through peak.`,
                    coach_r4_incentives:    `<strong>Paying people to cooperate buys the behaviour, not the belief.</strong> A dispatch bonus lifts the number this quarter, but the moment it stops, the legacy fracture is still there. Cooperation earned through justice is the only kind that lasts.`,
                    coach_r4_monitor:       `<strong>Tighter supervision enforces cooperation — which means you don't have it.</strong> Monitoring handoffs treats the symptom and signals distrust right back at a workforce already short on fairness. You can't supervise two legacies into becoming one team.`,

