                    insight_oneteam:        `One team — cooperation restored. You diagnosed a justice problem, not an operations one; communicated honestly; spent your fairness signals on procedure and interaction; ran a fair, exemplary redundancy; and stayed even-handed across legacies. Cooperation pulled dispatch back to the client commitment and the most mobile talent stayed.`,
                    insight_fractured:      `Justice breach — cooperation spent. Visibly favouring one legacy confirmed the south's worst fear and re-opened the fracture. The fairness gap is what your failing handoffs are made of; no amount of supervision closes it.`,
                    insight_partial:        `Partial recovery. The hub is no longer in free-fall, but the two workforces are not yet one team — the fairness gap and the cooperation index still tell you the integration is unfinished.`,
                    insight_contract:       `Dispatch is still below 90% — through peak that triggers the e-retailer's exit clause, the €8M/year contract is lost, and the merger's headline synergy case unravels. You cannot supervise your way to the number; only cooperation gets you there.`,
                    insight_dispatch:       `Dispatch is back at the 95% client commitment — and it held because cooperation recovered, not because you added hands. That is the whole point: the operational number is downstream of the justice work.`,
                    insight_rushed:         `You rushed the redundancy to bank synergy early and spent the procedural fairness that keeps survivors cooperating. Speed on the outcome is the most expensive saving you can make in a change.`,
                    insight_noexemplar:     `You never visibly modelled the behaviour you asked of others. In a merger, exemplarity is the loudest and cheapest signal available — and you left it on the table.`,
                    insight_turnover:       `Turnover risk is still high: the most mobile, most valuable people — experienced drivers and dispatchers — are the likeliest to go, and each trained driver costs ~€14k to replace. A fairness breach is a retention breach.`,
                    insight_balanced:       `The fairness gap between legacies is closed and cooperation is high — perceived even-handedness across the two workforces is exactly what converts a contested merger into a cooperating organisation.`,

                    hint_dispatch:          `Dispatch is below the 90% exit-clause line. Adding hands won't lift it — cooperation will. Repair the justice signals and the handoffs follow.`,
                    hint_favour:            `You've favoured one legacy and the gap has widened to a danger zone. Even-handedness across legacies in the first 90 days outweighs any number you think you're protecting.`,
                    hint_rushed:            `You rushed the cuts and skipped voice. Procedural fairness is what keeps the survivors cooperating — re-open voice and lead the recovery on process, not speed.`,
                    hint_exemplar:          `You haven't visibly modelled the behaviour you're asking for. Deliver the hard news yourself, share the pain, and apply criteria evenly — exemplarity is your cheapest lever.`,
                    hint_turnover:          `Turnover intent is climbing and the most mobile talent goes first. Protect the psychological contract with visible fairness, or you lose the people the hub can't replace.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — cooperation, fairness and dispatch are all healthy. Lock it in with an honest scorecard and a norms-based operating model to hold it through peak.`,

